    {
      "id": "normal",
      "kind": "normal",
      "title": "Normal: counting stem cells by CD34",
      "start": 20,
      "end": 60,
      "altText": "Flow cytometry: cells from the product pass a laser one at a time; antibodies against CD34 light up the rare progenitor cells (shown as a few highlighted dots among many). The CD34⁺ count per microlitre is multiplied by the product volume and divided by the patient’s weight. Worked example: 4.2 × 10⁸ CD34⁺ cells in the bag ÷ 70 kg = 6.0 × 10⁶ CD34⁺ cells/kg.",
      "cues": [
        {
          "id": "n1",
          "start": 20,
          "end": 29,
          "text": "Hematopoietic progenitor cells carry a surface marker called CD34. The lab counts them by flow cytometry, using a standardized gating method."
        },
        {
          "id": "n2",
          "start": 29,
          "end": 38,
          "text": "Cells pass through a laser one by one. Those labelled with anti-CD34 light up — and they are rare among all the cells in the bag."
        },
        {
          "id": "n3",
          "start": 38,
          "end": 49,
          "text": "Multiply the CD34-positive concentration by the product volume to get the total in the bag, then divide by the patient’s weight in kilograms."
        },
        {
          "id": "n4",
          "start": 49,
          "end": 60,
          "text": "Example: 4.2 times ten to the eighth CD34-positive cells, divided by 70 kilograms, gives 6.0 times ten to the sixth per kilogram."
        }
      ],
