    {
      "id": "change",
      "kind": "change",
      "title": "What changes: the minimum dose",
      "start": 60,
      "end": 100,
      "altText": "A dose bar from 0 to 6 × 10⁶ CD34⁺/kg. A red line marks the minimum threshold for autologous transplantation, ≥ 2 × 10⁶ CD34⁺/kg (the reviewer gives 2.5 × 10⁶). The example product (6.0) sits safely above. A poor mobilizer’s product (1.1) sits below and is flagged “collect more / remobilize”. A note: mobilization failures with G-CSF alone range from about 8% (myeloma) to 25% (non-Hodgkin lymphoma); adding plerixafor raises collection yields.",
      "cues": [
        {
          "id": "c1",
          "start": 60,
          "end": 70,
          "text": "Too few stem cells and the marrow may not recover. A commonly cited minimum for autologous transplantation is at least two million CD34-positive cells per kilogram."
        },
        {
          "id": "c2",
          "start": 70,
          "end": 79,
          "text": "Some references, including the reviewer, use two and a half million. Check your program’s threshold."
        },
        {
          "id": "c3",
          "start": 79,
          "end": 89,
          "text": "Our example, six million per kilogram, is comfortably above the line. A poor collection, say 1.1 million, is below it — the team must collect more or mobilize again."
        },
        {
          "id": "c4",
          "start": 89,
          "end": 100,
          "text": "Mobilization with G-CSF alone fails in roughly 8 percent of myeloma patients and up to 25 percent with non-Hodgkin lymphoma. Adding plerixafor improves the yield."
        }
      ],
