  "explanation": {
    "what": "Plain language: a stem-cell bag is judged by how many stem cells it holds for each kilogram of the patient. Scientific terms: hematopoietic progenitor cells are enumerated as CD34⁺ cells by flow cytometry (ISHAGE-based gating); the dose is CD34⁺ cells per kg of recipient weight; a minimum of ≥ 2 × 10⁶ CD34⁺/kg is commonly cited for autologous transplantation.",
    "why": "Too low a dose risks failed or delayed engraftment. G-CSF mobilization fails in roughly 8% (myeloma) to 25% (non-Hodgkin lymphoma) of patients; plerixafor improves yields. Cryoprotectants such as DMSO allow water to leave cells and prevent intracellular ice, but are toxic at high concentrations.",
    "measure": "Dose = (CD34⁺/µL × product volume) ÷ recipient weight. Cryopreservation: DMSO (commonly 10%), slow controlled-rate freezing (≈1–2 °C/min for hematopoietic cells), storage in liquid or vapour-phase nitrogen, rapid thaw.",
    "differs": "CD34⁺ dose (adequacy) vs total nucleated count; slow freezing for hematopoietic cells vs rapid cooling/vitrification for oocytes and embryonic stem cells.",
    "examAngle": "Expect: dose calculation, the minimum threshold, poor mobilization, DMSO as cryoprotectant and slow controlled-rate freezing."
  },
